1
00:00:00,137 --> 00:00:10,588
This is team Divinecodes, presenting Project Two: mileage forecasting and erroneous mileage detection. We built

2
00:00:10,588 --> 00:00:18,941
a local pipeline and an interactive demonstration to estimate a rental car's current mileage, forecast future mileage, and identify reports that deserve a closer look

3
00:00:18,938 --> 00:00:26,475
During a long rental, the latest recorded odometer may be weeks out of date. SIXT

4
00:00:26,475 --> 00:00:31,208
still needs to value the car and decide when to remove it from the rental

5
00:00:31,208 --> 00:00:43,034
fleet. A nowcast estimates mileage today. A forecast estimates mileage on a future date. Suspicious reading detection helps analysts find possible entry mistakes or unusual usage

6
00:00:42,223 --> 00:00:50,222
The development sample contains nearly two point seven million status rows across twenty seven thousand

7
00:00:50,222 --> 00:00:59,860
six hundred and thirty vehicles. We added Parquet support and streaming fleet loading. The separate

8
00:00:59,860 --> 00:01:08,906
inference file has one hundred and sixty three thousand, forty seven missing mileage targets. We

9
00:01:08,906 --> 00:01:15,730
keep those targets separate from model tuning and do not use the empty organizer prediction or error columns as features

10
00:01:09,308 --> 00:01:18,367
Each row is a vehicle status update. The anonymous vehicle identifier groups the history. The

11
00:01:18,367 --> 00:01:25,139
timestamp tells us when a row was recorded, while the mileage field contains the stored

12
00:01:25,139 --> 00:01:36,982
odometer. Rental status, handover, return date and rental product describe the rental context. Vehicle category

13
00:01:36,982 --> 00:01:46,441
and planned utilisation help estimate usage. The maximum mileage field is a business limit, not

14
00:01:46,441 --> 00:01:54,154
a substitute for an actual reading. Some descriptive fields are available in the source without being used by every model

15
00:01:44,516 --> 00:01:55,263
The loader supports CSV and Parquet, validates required fields, groups observations by vehicle, removes duplicate

16
00:01:55,263 --> 00:02:04,067
readings, and combines rental coverage. Missing or non finite mileage values are not treated as

17
00:02:04,067 --> 00:02:11,515
real readings. The fleet audit found more than six hundred thousand rows with return dates

18
00:02:11,515 --> 00:02:19,183
later than their recorded timestamps, so a return field can be a plan. The stale

19
00:02:19,183 --> 00:02:29,762
handover pattern also has exceptions. These findings are documented as assumptions and validation questions, not hidden by the pipeline

20
00:02:19,714 --> 00:02:24,756
Our main insight is that the date a mileage value belongs to can differ from

21
00:02:24,756 --> 00:02:33,444
the date the system first knows it. Some On Rent rows contain older handover mileage

22
00:02:33,444 --> 00:02:41,195
We retime those values, but never reveal them before their row was recorded. We apply

23
00:02:41,195 --> 00:02:50,037
the same availability boundary to rental metadata and changing vehicle categories. The fleet pattern is not universal, so we document the assumption rather than treating every row as certain truth

24
00:02:48,063 --> 00:02:56,516
The simplest baseline keeps the last reading unchanged. The average rate baseline adds the vehicle's

25
00:02:56,516 --> 00:03:05,430
historical daily mileage multiplied by the gap. Our rental aware model estimates separate driving rates

26
00:03:05,430 --> 00:03:12,891
for rented days and idle days. It adds each rate multiplied by the corresponding number

27
00:03:12,891 --> 00:03:23,915
of days. Unknown days after the last recorded status use planned utilisation. For future dates

28
00:03:23,915 --> 00:03:33,404
the model estimates remaining rental exposure from available rental history and then the utilisation plan Recorded defleet status stops planning new rentals

29
00:03:25,005 --> 00:03:34,188
Sparse vehicle histories borrow strength from broader product and vehicle category groups. The recent rental

30
00:03:34,188 --> 00:03:43,937
aware variant downweights older usage. The blend averages rental aware and average rate predictions. The

31
00:03:43,937 --> 00:03:54,126
robust candidate cleans already observed isolated typos and clips historical interval rates. All six candidates

32
00:03:54,126 --> 00:04:02,379
are measured on the same targets. Earlier validation selects recent rental aware for current mileage

33
00:04:02,379 --> 00:04:10,998
and the blend for forecasts. More complexity does not automatically win: the plain rental aware

34
00:04:10,998 --> 00:04:17,794
model is worse than average rate for forecast error, and the robust candidate does not win the selected tasks

35
00:04:05,876 --> 00:04:14,171
We compared the last recorded reading, an average daily rate, rental aware models, a recent

36
00:04:14,171 --> 00:04:23,823
usage variant, a blend, and a robust rate candidate. Earlier validation chooses the model separately

37
00:04:23,823 --> 00:04:34,150
for each task. A different later period calibrates the prediction ranges. Final evaluation uses later

38
00:04:34,150 --> 00:04:42,917
readings on held out vehicles. Future field mutation tests check that changing future rows cannot change earlier predictions

39
00:04:35,445 --> 00:04:42,520
The stable hash split reserves five thousand, five hundred and fifty nine vehicles for final

40
00:04:42,520 --> 00:04:51,752
testing and uses twenty two thousand, seventy one for training fleet priors. Initial training ends

41
00:04:51,752 --> 00:05:01,081
on March thirty first, twenty twenty five. Model selection uses later training vehicle targets through

42
00:05:01,081 --> 00:05:11,911
July twenty first. A separate subsequent period through November eleventh calibrates ranges. Final models fit

43
00:05:11,911 --> 00:05:19,537
at that cutoff and are evaluated afterwards on held out vehicles. Held out vehicles may

44
00:05:19,537 --> 00:05:29,116
supply their own earlier history, but their outcomes never train fleet priors. Suspicious test targets are retained rather than removed to improve the score

45
00:05:18,006 --> 00:05:25,247
Mean absolute error, or M A E, is the average absolute distance between the estimate

46
00:05:25,247 --> 00:05:34,176
and recorded target. It is our primary selection metric. M A P E expresses error

47
00:05:34,176 --> 00:05:41,644
as a percentage of the odometer, using a minimum denominator of one kilometre, so small

48
00:05:41,644 --> 00:05:48,981
or erroneous readings can dominate it. W A P E compares error with the distance

49
00:05:48,981 --> 00:05:59,308
driven since the last known reading. Bias shows whether estimates systematically overshoot or undershoot. Skill

50
00:05:59,308 --> 00:06:07,896
measures improvement over the last reading baseline. Nowcasts use a rolling origin one day before a return source target; forecasts use the fixed cutoff

51
00:05:58,968 --> 00:06:05,430
The selected current mileage model has an odometer percentage error of seven point two eight

52
00:06:05,430 --> 00:06:13,966
percent, compared with nine point five four percent for the last reading baseline. Its signed

53
00:06:13,966 --> 00:06:22,460
bias is about minus one hundred and forty four kilometres. The selected forecast has percentage

54
00:06:22,460 --> 00:06:29,701
error of seventeen point three one percent, compared with thirty eight point zero one percent

55
00:06:29,701 --> 00:06:38,196
for the last reading baseline, and bias of about plus one hundred and fourteen kilometres

56
00:06:38,196 --> 00:06:47,617
Distance based weighted error is also reported. These complementary metrics help reveal systematic behaviour that a single average can hide

57
00:06:36,790 --> 00:06:45,347
Here is the working dashboard. Every vehicle in this demonstration is made up. Select a

58
00:06:45,347 --> 00:06:52,126
car on the left. The first card shows its last recorded mileage and the age

59
00:06:52,126 --> 00:07:00,614
of that reading. The orange card shows the model's current estimate and a prediction range

60
00:07:00,614 --> 00:07:07,327
The timeline connects the known history to the estimate, making the gap visible instead of hiding the uncertainty

61
00:07:01,315 --> 00:07:09,893
Next, change the forecast horizon from thirty days to ninety days. The future estimate and

62
00:07:09,893 --> 00:07:18,876
its range update using outputs precomputed by the Python models. Longer horizons can require much

63
00:07:18,876 --> 00:07:28,463
wider ranges. The page also supports searching, filtering, and exporting the selected estimate as a CSV. The demo works offline and needs no API key

64
00:07:26,471 --> 00:07:35,241
The ranges are calibrated from earlier prediction errors, separately from choosing the model. Their width

65
00:07:35,241 --> 00:07:41,131
scales with the square root of the time since the last reading, with separate gap

66
00:07:41,131 --> 00:07:51,665
buckets where enough calibration examples exist. The target is ninety percent coverage. Measured current mileage

67
00:07:51,665 --> 00:07:59,347
coverage is ninety point seven percent, with mean full width of one thousand, six hundred

68
00:07:59,347 --> 00:08:08,324
and eleven kilometres. Forecast coverage is ninety five point six percent, with a much wider

69
00:08:08,324 --> 00:08:18,448
mean range. These are measured results, not guarantees under fleet shifts, temporal change, or dependent repeated readings

70
00:08:06,343 --> 00:08:14,886
The review queue includes an intentionally invented mileage spike. Each signal shows the report date

71
00:08:14,886 --> 00:08:25,041
the reading, and a reason for investigation. Our rules cover isolated spikes or dips, decreases

72
00:08:25,041 --> 00:08:37,118
implausible rates, movement without recorded rentals, unchanged readings during rental days, and model residual outliers

73
00:08:37,118 --> 00:08:43,784
A flag is a request for review. It is not proof of fraud, and real precision still requires domain expert labels

74
00:08:37,327 --> 00:08:44,216
An isolated spike or dip is identified between consistent neighbours and removed before the other

75
00:08:44,216 --> 00:08:51,808
rules run, so one typo does not create a cascade. A decrease is more than

76
00:08:51,808 --> 00:08:58,704
ten kilometres below an earlier maximum and is reported once per episode unless it drops

77
00:08:58,704 --> 00:09:08,528
further. An implausible rate exceeds fifteen hundred kilometres a day. Movement without a recorded rental

78
00:09:08,528 --> 00:09:17,187
exceeds three hundred kilometres. Unchanged fresh return readings are checked after at least three rental

79
00:09:17,187 --> 00:09:24,359
days. Residual outliers need a robust z score above eight and at least a thousand

80
00:09:24,359 --> 00:09:34,683
kilometres of disagreement. Some rules need later neighbours, so the queue is retrospective. Same day differences alone are not errors

81
00:09:21,877 --> 00:09:29,186
On held out development readings, current mileage error falls from five hundred and sixty one

82
00:09:29,186 --> 00:09:37,543
kilometres for the last reading baseline to four hundred and fifteen. Forecast error falls from

83
00:09:37,543 --> 00:09:45,824
seven thousand, three hundred and seventy six kilometres to two thousand, seven hundred and thirty

84
00:09:45,824 --> 00:09:54,663
four. Those are reductions of twenty six percent and sixty two point nine percent. The

85
00:09:54,663 --> 00:10:04,595
selected models also beat the stronger average rate baseline. These are fleet evaluation results, separate from the synthetic dashboard

86
00:09:55,461 --> 00:10:03,735
Overall averages can hide difficult cases. Long term current mileage error is five hundred and

87
00:10:03,735 --> 00:10:12,160
twenty seven kilometres on only one hundred and five targets. Long term forecast error is

88
00:10:12,160 --> 00:10:20,737
three thousand, seven hundred and ninety six on one hundred and forty targets. Current mileage

89
00:10:20,737 --> 00:10:28,371
error beyond a sixty day stale reading gap reaches five thousand and forty nine kilometres

90
00:10:28,371 --> 00:10:37,982
on only twelve targets. Those small groups need caution. The report separates stale reading gap

91
00:10:37,982 --> 00:10:46,149
buckets from future lead time buckets. Historical mode of product is not a perfect current contract label, and this sample is dominated by short term rentals

92
00:10:37,542 --> 00:10:44,411
In a controlled development benchmark, the detector caught four hundred and fifty one of four

93
00:10:44,411 --> 00:10:54,469
hundred and seventy nine injected mistakes: ninety four point two percent episode recall. Exact corrupted

94
00:10:54,469 --> 00:11:02,736
reading recall was ninety three point nine percent. Episode recall can also credit the reading

95
00:11:02,736 --> 00:11:12,846
immediately before a rollback. This does not establish real world precision. Untouched readings may already contain errors, and some flags may reflect missing or stale operational data

96
00:11:08,890 --> 00:11:17,384
The injected error benchmark uses fixed random seeds on a deterministic development cohort, restricted to

97
00:11:17,384 --> 00:11:27,505
training vehicles and rows recorded by February first, twenty twenty five. Detection catches every injected

98
00:11:27,505 --> 00:11:37,170
extra digit, about ninety seven percent of missing digits, ninety percent of transposed digits, ninety

99
00:11:37,170 --> 00:11:46,443
nine percent of rollback episodes, and seventy nine percent of stuck readings. Small digit changes

100
00:11:46,443 --> 00:11:57,911
can resemble ordinary driving. These controlled results test particular mistake types, not every real anomaly Thresholds were not selected using final held out outcomes

101
00:11:48,997 --> 00:11:55,707
The full fleet scan flags eighty four point two two readings per thousand, so review

102
00:11:55,707 --> 00:12:06,276
workload matters. Across benchmark seeds, five thousand, nine hundred and sixty five untouched readings are

103
00:12:06,276 --> 00:12:16,638
flagged. They may contain existing errors, missing rental records, or stale data artifacts, so this

104
00:12:16,638 --> 00:12:24,154
is not a measured false positive rate. Comparing against the original flag set gives one

105
00:12:24,154 --> 00:12:33,117
hundred and twenty two additional untouched flags after injection. The local review file includes neighbouring

106
00:12:33,117 --> 00:12:41,656
readings and rental context. Human adjudication is still pending, and we do not claim real precision

107
00:12:28,944 --> 00:12:36,860
The aggregate report also breaks down the full fleet review workload by rule. Movement without

108
00:12:36,860 --> 00:12:45,534
a recorded rental and unchanged readings contribute many signals, and require care because operational records

109
00:12:45,534 --> 00:12:56,191
can be incomplete or stale. Isolated typos, implausible rates, decreases and residual disagreements contribute the

110
00:12:56,191 --> 00:13:03,976
remaining signals. A reading can trigger more than one rule, so the signal total is

111
00:13:03,976 --> 00:13:13,191
not a count of independently confirmed bad readings. The local context review helps analysts distinguish likely entry errors, unusual but plausible usage, and possible pipeline artifacts

112
00:13:07,991 --> 00:13:15,115
We filled all one hundred and sixty three thousand, forty seven missing mileage targets and

113
00:13:15,115 --> 00:13:23,981
preserved the original table. Most use the vehicle's own earlier history with a range. Three

114
00:13:23,981 --> 00:13:31,263
thousand, seven hundred and forty five have no usable own history, so they receive an

115
00:13:31,263 --> 00:13:41,477
explicitly flagged fleet cohort fallback. Those fallbacks have no calibrated range and need review. The error column stays unchanged because the masked ground truth is unavailable

116
00:13:37,595 --> 00:13:45,104
The completed Parquet file preserves all six hundred and fifty six thousand, six hundred and

117
00:13:45,104 --> 00:13:54,440
one rows and the original columns. Observed history ends on July first, twenty twenty six

118
00:13:54,440 --> 00:14:02,927
missing targets run from July second to November eleventh. Targets after a vehicle's latest recorded

119
00:14:02,927 --> 00:14:11,463
status use the forecast model from that status date. Fleet priors and calibration use earlier

120
00:14:11,463 --> 00:14:21,314
quarter start snapshots from a bounded thousand vehicle development cohort. Cold start fallbacks use category

121
00:14:21,314 --> 00:14:29,578
and model year where supported. They are not validated by the history based accuracy table have no calibrated range, and require review. No masked target accuracy is claimed

122
00:14:22,801 --> 00:14:34,251
The project includes reproducible commands, measured aggregate evidence, thirty one passing tests, an offline dashboard

123
00:14:34,251 --> 00:14:42,559
and a five slide presentation. A clean source copy runs the public example without organizer

124
00:14:42,559 --> 00:14:53,321
data. The next step is a shadow mode pilot: confirm measurement semantics, label representative flags

125
00:14:53,321 --> 00:15:01,867
and validate long term rentals and range coverage. We demonstrate a working decision aid, not proven fraud detection, guaranteed savings, or production readiness

126
00:14:56,194 --> 00:15:08,478
The main implementation is mileage dot py. It contains timeline construction, prediction models, backtesting, uncertainty

127
00:15:08,478 --> 00:15:19,529
and flags. Run baseline is the evaluation entry point, and profile data produces aggregate checks

128
00:15:19,529 --> 00:15:30,207
Flag benchmark injects known mistakes, while review flags creates contextual triage material. Predict missing writes

129
00:15:30,207 --> 00:15:41,375
inference outputs, and test mileage checks correctness and information boundaries. Synthetic fleet and synthetic evaluation

130
00:15:41,375 --> 00:15:50,968
scripts support method checks; their older archived results are not presented as current fleet performance The interactive dashboard is generated from actual Python outputs on made up vehicles

131
00:15:41,955 --> 00:15:51,276
From the Project Two folder, create and activate a virtual environment, then install requirements. The

132
00:15:51,276 --> 00:15:58,813
model code needs numpy, and Duck D B reads Parquet. Run the unit tests and

133
00:15:58,813 --> 00:16:08,355
the public example before supplying organizer data explicitly. Run baseline evaluates the fleet and saves

134
00:16:08,355 --> 00:16:19,756
aggregate metrics; predict missing produces the completed inference table. Build demo creates the offline dashboard

135
00:16:19,756 --> 00:16:31,709
Exact platform commands, flag benchmark options, date specific nowcast and forecast exports, and reproduction instructions

136
00:16:31,709 --> 00:16:39,167
are in the submission. The measured full pipeline batch took about eleven hundred seconds on this machine, not milliseconds per request

137
00:16:27,551 --> 00:16:34,826
A daily batch could give valuation analysts estimated mileage, the age of the last reading

138
00:16:34,826 --> 00:16:44,561
and uncertainty. Defleet planners could use future mileage and a separate mileage limit alert. Exceeding

139
00:16:44,561 --> 00:16:52,332
a contract or planning limit is a business alert, not automatically a data error. A

140
00:16:52,332 --> 00:17:01,529
review team should inspect the largest discrepancies with context, then confirm or reject them. Start

141
00:17:01,529 --> 00:17:09,462
in shadow mode and measure review workload and decision quality. We have not measured financial savings, and no monetary benefit is asserted without a SIXT study

142
00:17:03,048 --> 00:17:12,383
Several limitations remain. Retiming all On Rent values is a working interpretation, not a proven

143
00:17:12,383 --> 00:17:20,278
rule for every vehicle. Return source targets can be stale or wrong, and return days

144
00:17:20,278 --> 00:17:30,488
differ from arbitrary valuation days. Rolling nowcasts are one day ahead evaluation proxies, not direct

145
00:17:30,488 --> 00:17:40,746
observation of hidden daily mileage. Long term groups are small. Cold start fallbacks require separate

146
00:17:40,746 --> 00:17:51,425
validation. Calibrated ranges can shift over time, and flags need expert labels. A future pilot

147
00:17:51,425 --> 00:18:00,102
should confirm source semantics, obtain fresh long term readings, and monitor errors and coverage over time

148
00:17:45,990 --> 00:17:54,773
The official rubric allocates fifteen points to understanding the problem, twenty five to approach, twenty

149
00:17:54,773 --> 00:18:03,923
five to results, fifteen to business relevance, and ten each to code and presentation. These

150
00:18:03,923 --> 00:18:14,091
are the jury's maximums, not a score we award ourselves. The repository contains the implementation

151
00:18:14,091 --> 00:18:25,074
write up, aggregate evidence, five slide deck, short video and detailed walkthrough. Existing tracked organizer

152
00:18:25,074 --> 00:18:35,820
datasets still require publishing permission or exclusion, and repository rules require confirmation before a push

153
00:18:35,820 --> 00:18:43,092
This complete walkthrough explains the work; the separate short cut respects the one minute video submission limit
